#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# Published-skill immutability guard (K-744).
#
# WHAT THIS ENFORCES
# ------------------
# `skill-vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH.md` files at the root of this repo are PUBLISHED
# ARTEFACTS, and this repo publishes, in normative text, that they never
# change:
#
#   specification.html  "Because a version file is immutable, this pin cannot
#                        drift by construction."
#   spec/protocol.md    §14.4 "Published skill files are immutable and
#                        versioned; a change ships a new file."
#   CLAUDE.md           "a published version file is never edited, every change
#                        ships a new one"  (ADR-0012)
#
# An operator's `/.well-known/kiosk.json` may carry a `skill` pin: the versioned
# URL plus the SHA-256 of its bytes. An AI assistant fetches the URL FROM HERE
# and refuses the skill on a hash mismatch — fail-closed, by design. So editing
# a file that is already published does not "update" anything: it silently
# invalidates every pin taken against the previous bytes, and the only symptom
# is assistants declining to transact against operators who did exactly what the
# spec told them to do. The sentence above stops being true the moment it
# happens, and it is a sentence about a supply-chain control.
#
# WHY IT IS A CHECK AND NOT A CONVENTION
# --------------------------------------
# Because the convention already failed once. Commit 5a9fbf5 (2026-08-10) —
# whose stated purpose was to make a frozen skill's pin un-rottable — achieved
# it by rewriting two bullets inside the already-published skill-v0.3.10.md.
# The effect happened to be nil (no demo held the pre-edit hash), which is
# exactly why nothing caught it and why nothing would catch the next one. The
# reference repo's `bin/check-*` guards exist for the same reason; this is the
# same shape, in the repo that owns the files.
#
# THE SECOND INVARIANT: THE ALIAS MAY NOT COME TO REST DIVERGED (K-847)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# This guard's header used to say «editing skill.md (the alias) allowed — the
# working alias is mutable by design», and that sentence sanctioned a practice
# this repo has now retired. Under it, notes were staged in the live alias
# between cuts, and for as long as they sat there `specification.html` and
# `skill.md` were BOTH publishing a false sentence about a supply-chain control:
# «`https://kiosk.tech/skill.md` is the "latest" alias, byte-identical to the
# newest version file». It was false at head for days, and the drift widened
# while it was being decided.
#
# Phil's answer (2026-08-21, K-847) was to make the
# sentence TRUE rather than to soften it, and to state the discipline that keeps
# it true:
#
#   1. a skill edit ENDS in a version bump — the alias may not come to rest
#      diverged from the newest cut;
#   2. re-pinning the demos is PART of the change, not follow-up work;
#   3. no cut anything pins is EVER mutated — so when skill changes are
#      foreseen, bump FIRST and make the edits inside the new cut.
#
# WHAT IS ALLOWED AND WHAT IS NOT
# -------------------------------
#   ADDING skill-v0.4.4.md           allowed — that IS the prescribed mechanism
#   editing skill-v0.4.4.md          allowed while it is UNTRACKED (or staged-add
#     before it is committed          and not yet committed) — which is exactly
#                                    the window rule 3's «bump first, then edit
#                                    inside the new cut» works in. THE CUT IS
#                                    FROZEN THE MOMENT IT IS COMMITTED, because
#                                    that is the same wave in which the demos
#                                    are re-pinned to it (rule 2): from then on
#                                    something pins it. The RANGE pass is
#                                    correspondingly forgiving — a branch that
#                                    adds a cut in one commit and amends it in
#                                    the next still shows a net `A` against the
#                                    base, so a cut that has not reached the
#                                    base branch is not frozen against it.
#                                    Deliberately NOT keyed on "is it in the
#                                    base?" here: a local `origin/main` that
#                                    lags by three cuts would then exempt three
#                                    already-pinned files.
#   editing skill.md (the alias)     allowed WHILE WORKING — but the tree must
#                                    come to rest with the alias byte-identical
#                                    to the newest cut, so an alias edit is
#                                    finished only when the cut carries it too.
#   editing skill-v0.4.2.md          FAILS — it is on the base branch
#   deleting skill-v0.3.4.md         FAILS — unpublishing breaks live pins just
#                                    as thoroughly as rewriting
#   renaming a cut                   FAILS (seen as delete + add)
#   alias sha != newest cut sha      FAILS — the two sentences above are
#                                    normative text on a published site
#   alias frontmatter version !=     FAILS — same reason: the version an
#     the newest cut's version         assistant reads must name the bytes it got
#   a page saying "the current       FAILS — a stale version line is a claim,
#     skill is <not the newest>"       not a typo: it tells an adopter to pin a
#                                      superseded cut (K-863)
#   an example pin URL naming        FAILS — same reason; that URL is copied
#     anything but the newest cut
#   `skill-v0.4.1.md` named in       allowed — that is HISTORY, and it must keep
#     prose without the origin         its number forever
#
# THE THIRD INVARIANT: THE PROSE THAT NAMES THE CURRENT CUT (K-863)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Passes 1-3 hold the BYTES. None of them reads a sentence, so the relink
# inventory kept a hand-maintained half — and that half rotted three times in
# six days. See the pass-4 block below for the full account and for what is
# deliberately left unchecked.
#
# HOW IT DECIDES
# --------------
# Four passes, because the useful moment to be stopped is before the commit,
# the authoritative moment is in CI, and the last two properties are about the
# tree rather than about a diff:
#
#   1. WORKING TREE — `git status` for tracked, modified or deleted cuts. Stops
#      an edit locally. A no-op in CI, where the tree is clean. Untracked and
#      staged-add paths pass: that is a cut being written, not one being
#      rewritten.
#   2. RANGE — `git diff --name-status --no-renames <merge-base> <head>`, i.e.
#      what this push / pull request does to the base branch. Only status `A`
#      passes. `--no-renames` on purpose: a rename decomposes into D + A, and
#      the D is a failure we want to see rather than a status letter to special-
#      case.
#   3. ALIAS AT REST — sha256(skill.md) against sha256(newest cut), plus the
#      frontmatter `version` against that cut's version. Reads the files, not
#      git, so it is the same answer locally and in CI.
#   4. PROSE AT REST — every published sentence that states WHICH cut is
#      current, every example `https://kiosk.tech/skill-v*.md` pin, and the
#      cut table in skills.md, all against the cuts on disk. No version number
#      appears in this script: the expectations are derived, so the next bump
#      moves the gate with it.
#
# "AT REST" MEANS THE TREE THAT GETS PUSHED, not every intermediate commit, and
# that is forced rather than lenient: a cut is frozen the moment it is
# committed, so the cut is necessarily the LAST commit of a skill change and the
# commits before it hold an alias that has moved ahead of it. Run this on the
# head you are about to push.
#
# BASE RESOLUTION, in order: `--base <ref>`, then `$BASE_SHA` (what CI sets from
# `github.event.before` for a push and `pull_request.base.sha` for a PR), then
# `origin/main`. If none of them resolves to a commit this EXITS NON-ZERO and
# says so: a guard that cannot see its base must not report success. The
# all-zeros SHA GitHub sends when a branch is created is treated as "unset".
#
# Stdlib only, no network, no build. ~0.1 s. Requires unshallowed history
# (`actions/checkout` with `fetch-depth: 0`).
#
# Usage:
#   bin/check-skill-immutability                 # working tree + origin/main..HEAD
#   bin/check-skill-immutability --base <ref>    # explicit base
#   bin/check-skill-immutability --head <ref>    # explicit head (default HEAD)
#   bin/check-skill-immutability --list          # every published cut + its sha256
#   bin/check-skill-immutability --self-test     # prove pass 4 is red on a plant of
#                                                # every rule, green on the history it
#                                                # must leave alone, and non-vacuous here
#
# Pass 4 reads the working tree (`git ls-files` + untracked-not-ignored), so it
# answers about the tree in front of you, not about a range.

require "digest"
require "open3"

ROOT = File.expand_path("..", __dir__)
Dir.chdir(ROOT)

# A published cut: `skill-v<anything>.md` at the repo root. Deliberately looser
# than `\d+\.\d+\.\d+` — anything named like a published version file is
# protected, so a future naming shape is covered rather than silently exempt.
CUT = %r{\Askill-v[^/]+\.md\z}

# The "latest" alias. Two published sentences say it carries the newest cut's
# exact bytes; pass 3 is what makes that true rather than aspirational (K-847).
ALIAS = "skill.md"

# Every published cut, oldest first. Sorted NUMERICALLY on the version
# components, not lexically — `skill-v0.3.10.md` is newer than `skill-v0.3.9.md`
# and a string sort says the opposite.
def cuts
  Dir["skill-v*.md"].select { CUT.match?(_1) }.sort_by { |f| f.scan(/\d+/).map(&:to_i) }
end

# The `version:` line of a skill file's YAML frontmatter, unquoted, or nil.
def frontmatter_version(path)
  File.read(path)[/^version:\s*"?([^"\n]+)"?\s*$/, 1]&.strip
end

# `-c log.follow=false` is not decoration. With `log.follow=true` in a user's
# global config (it is, on the machine this repo is maintained from), `git log
# -- skill-v0.3.11.md` follows the path back through the copy from skill.md and
# reports the repo's INITIAL commit as the one that added it. A guard that
# reads git must not inherit the reader's preferences.
def git(*args)
  out, err, status = Open3.capture3("git", "-c", "log.follow=false", *args)
  [out, err, status.success?]
end

def rev_parse(ref)
  return nil if ref.nil? || ref.empty?
  return nil if ref =~ /\A0{7,40}\z/ # GitHub's "no previous commit" sentinel

  out, _err, ok = git("rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", "#{ref}^{commit}")
  ok ? out.strip : nil
end

# ── argv ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
base_arg  = nil
head_arg  = "HEAD"
list      = false
self_test = false
argv      = ARGV.dup
until argv.empty?
  case (arg = argv.shift)
  when "--base" then base_arg = argv.shift
  when "--head" then head_arg = argv.shift
  when "--list" then list = true
  when "--self-test" then self_test = true
  else
    warn "usage: #{$PROGRAM_NAME} [--base <ref>] [--head <ref>] [--list] [--self-test]"
    exit 2
  end
end

# ── --list: the immutability ledger ──────────────────────────────────────────
if list
  published = cuts
  puts format("%-22s %-10s %-12s %s", "PUBLISHED CUT", "ADDED", "COMMIT", "SHA-256")
  published.each do |cut|
    log, _err, _ok = git("log", "--diff-filter=A", "--format=%h %ad", "--date=short", "--", cut)
    commit, date = log.lines.last.to_s.split(" ", 2).then { |h, d| [h.to_s, d.to_s.strip] }
    puts format("%-22s %-10s %-12s %s", cut, date, commit, Digest::SHA256.hexdigest(File.binread(cut)))
  end
  if File.exist?(ALIAS)
    puts format("%-22s %-10s %-12s %s", ALIAS, "(alias)", "-", Digest::SHA256.hexdigest(File.binread(ALIAS)))
  end
  puts
  puts "#{published.size} published cuts. Every one of them is frozen: a change ships a NEW file, " \
       "and #{ALIAS} carries the newest cut's exact bytes."
  puts
end

failures    = []
notes       = []
newest_cut  = cuts.last

# ── Base + head resolution ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
env_base = ENV["BASE_SHA"] || ENV["GITHUB_BASE_SHA"]
base_src, base =
  if base_arg then ["--base #{base_arg}", rev_parse(base_arg)]
  elsif rev_parse(env_base) then ["$BASE_SHA (#{env_base[0, 12]})", rev_parse(env_base)]
  else ["origin/main", rev_parse("origin/main")]
  end

head = rev_parse(head_arg)

# ── Pass 1: the working tree ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `git status --porcelain` XY codes: an untracked file is `??` (a new cut being
# written — allowed), `A` is staged-add (allowed). Anything else on a path that
# matches CUT is a modification or a deletion of something already tracked.
status_out, status_err, status_ok = git("status", "--porcelain", "--untracked-files=all")
if status_ok
  status_out.each_line do |line|
    code = line[0, 2].to_s
    path = line[3..].to_s.strip.delete_prefix('"').delete_suffix('"')
    # Rename entries read `R  old -> new`; the old path is the one at risk.
    path = path.split(" -> ").first.to_s
    next unless CUT.match?(path)
    next if code == "??"            # a brand-new cut, not yet tracked
    next if code.start_with?("A")   # staged add of a brand-new cut

    verb = code.include?("D") ? "DELETED" : "MODIFIED"
    failures << "working tree: #{path} is #{verb} (git status `#{code.strip}`) — that file is " \
                "already published and every pin taken against it verifies its CURRENT bytes. " \
                "Revert it (`git checkout -- #{path}`) and ship the change as a NEW cut."
  end
else
  failures << "could not read `git status` (#{status_err.strip}) — this guard must not pass " \
              "without having looked."
end

# ── Pass 2: the range this push / PR introduces ──────────────────────────────
if head.nil?
  failures << "could not resolve head ref `#{head_arg}` to a commit."
elsif base.nil?
  failures << "could not resolve a base commit (tried #{base_src}). This guard compares what a " \
              "push or pull request DOES to the base branch, so with no base it can see nothing " \
              "and must not report success. Pass one explicitly: " \
              "`bin/check-skill-immutability --base <ref>`. In CI, set BASE_SHA (and check out " \
              "with fetch-depth: 0 so the base commit is present)."
else
  # merge-base, not the raw base: correct for a fast-forward push (base itself),
  # for a PR merge commit (the base tip), and for a diverged or force-pushed
  # branch, where a raw two-dot diff would report the base branch's own commits
  # as if this branch had made them.
  mb_out, _err, mb_ok = git("merge-base", base, head)
  from = mb_ok ? mb_out.strip : base
  notes << "no merge-base between #{base[0, 12]} and #{head[0, 12]} — diffing from the base " \
           "directly" unless mb_ok

  diff_out, diff_err, diff_ok = git("diff", "--name-status", "--no-renames", from, head)
  if diff_ok
    changed = diff_out.lines.filter_map do |line|
      code, path = line.chomp.split("\t", 2)
      next unless path && CUT.match?(path)

      [code.to_s[0], path]
    end

    changed.each do |code, path|
      next if code == "A" # publishing a NEW cut is the prescribed mechanism

      verb = case code
             when "D" then "DELETES"
             when "M" then "MODIFIES"
             else "changes (git status `#{code}`)"
             end
      failures << "#{from[0, 12]}..#{head[0, 12]} #{verb} the already-published #{path}. " \
                  "Published skill cuts are immutable (protocol.md §14.4, specification.html: " \
                  "\"this pin cannot drift by construction\"): an operator's `skill` pin verifies " \
                  "that file's SHA-256, so changing or removing it breaks every assistant holding " \
                  "the old hash — fail-closed and invisible from the operator's side. Ship the " \
                  "change as a NEW skill-v*.md and re-pin, exactly as ADR-0012 prescribes."
    end

    added = changed.count { |code, _| code == "A" }
    notes << "range #{from[0, 12]}..#{head[0, 12]}: #{added} new cut(s) published, " \
             "#{changed.size - added} existing cut(s) touched"
  else
    failures << "could not diff #{from}..#{head} (#{diff_err.strip}) — if this is CI, check out " \
                "with `fetch-depth: 0`; a shallow clone does not contain the base commit."
  end
end

# ── Pass 3: the alias, at rest (K-847) ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# The two sentences this enforces are NORMATIVE text on a published site:
#   specification.html  "`https://kiosk.tech/skill.md` is the "latest" alias,
#                        byte-identical to the newest version file"
#   skill.md            "`https://kiosk.tech/skill.md` is the "latest" alias
#                        with identical content"
# Phil's K-847 answer was to keep them and make them true, which turns them into
# a build invariant: a skill edit is not finished until a cut carries it and the
# alias equals that cut. Reads the FILES, so it says the same thing locally and
# in CI, and it does not care which commit put them there.
if File.exist?(ALIAS)
  if newest_cut.nil?
    failures << "no skill-v*.md cut exists at all, so #{ALIAS} mirrors nothing."
  else
    alias_sha = Digest::SHA256.hexdigest(File.binread(ALIAS))
    cut_sha   = Digest::SHA256.hexdigest(File.binread(newest_cut))
    if alias_sha == cut_sha
      notes << "alias #{ALIAS} == newest cut #{newest_cut} (#{alias_sha[0, 12]}…)"
    else
      failures << "#{ALIAS} is NOT byte-identical to the newest cut #{newest_cut} " \
                  "(#{alias_sha[0, 12]}… vs #{cut_sha[0, 12]}…). specification.html and " \
                  "#{ALIAS} itself both PUBLISH that it is, about a supply-chain control, so " \
                  "the tree must not come to rest like this. A skill edit ends in a version " \
                  "bump (K-847): cut a new skill-v*.md from the alias, re-pin every consumer, " \
                  "and never edit a cut once it has been committed."
    end

    alias_version = frontmatter_version(ALIAS)
    cut_version   = newest_cut[/skill-v(.+)\.md\z/, 1]
    if alias_version != cut_version
      failures << "#{ALIAS} frontmatter says version #{alias_version.inspect} but the newest cut " \
                  "is #{cut_version.inspect} (#{newest_cut}). An assistant reads the version out " \
                  "of the frontmatter, so it must name the bytes it was handed."
    end
  end
else
  failures << "#{ALIAS} does not exist — the \"latest\" alias is part of what this site publishes."
end

# ── Pass 4: the PROSE that names the current cut (K-863, K-745) ──────────────
#
# Passes 1–3 hold the ARTEFACTS: no published cut is rewritten, and the alias
# carries the newest cut's exact bytes. None of them reads a sentence. So the
# relink inventory kept its hand-maintained half, and that half rotted three
# times in six days: `CLAUDE.md` sat three cuts behind (K-863), `protocol.md`
# §14.4 said «The current skill is 0.4.2» while `specification.html` two files
# away already said 0.4.3 (K-745), and the illustrative `skill` pin in
# `specification.html` and in the schema examples named `skill-v0.4.0.md`,
# three cuts stale.
#
# A stale version line on the normative page is a CLAIM, not a typo: it tells
# an adopter to pin a superseded cut, and the dual-check will happily verify
# that superseded cut's bytes and then drive a wire the operator no longer
# serves. So the prose gets a gate for the same reason the bytes do.
#
# EVERYTHING HERE IS DERIVED FROM THE FILES ON DISK. There is no version number
# in this script — the expectations come from `cuts`, so the next bump moves the
# gate with it and nobody has to remember to edit the guard.
#
# WHAT IS CHECKED
#
#   R1  a sentence that states WHICH cut is current names the newest cut
#       («The current skill is X», «currently skill-vX», «current skill-vX.md»,
#        «a X operator no longer serves»)
#   R2  «A-B describe earlier <series> wires» spans the newest series from its
#       first cut to the one immediately before the newest
#   R3  «A-B (cuts) describe protocol S-T» spans the oldest cut to the last cut
#       before the newest series
#   R4  every `https://kiosk.tech/skill-vX.Y.Z.md` URL — a PIN, something a
#       reader copies — names the newest cut
#   R5  every `skill-vX.Y.Z.md` named anywhere names a cut that EXISTS
#   R6  the cut table in skills.md carries a row for every cut on the newest
#       series, newest first
#   R7  the cut table's «same wire» chain («0.4.3 = 0.4.4 = … = 0.4.10») is a
#       contiguous run of published cuts and reaches the newest one
#   R8  the PER-CUT HISTORY CLAUSE LIST («0.4.0–0.4.2 on wires that have since
#       changed, 0.4.3 on this same wire but …, … and 0.4.9 on this same wire
#       but …») names every cut on the newest series BELOW the newest, exactly
#       once, in order — ranges expanded through the cuts on disk (K-1232)
#   R9  each cut table ROW BODY: its protocol column states the series its own
#       cut is on, and a «the same wire as X» in it names that cut's immediate
#       PREDECESSOR (K-1232)
#   R10 the same-wire group written as a SPAN rather than as a chain («the
#       "same wire" chain below runs 0.4.3 → 0.4.10 only») reaches the newest
#       cut, exactly as R7 requires of the «=» spelling (K-1232)
#   R11 the per-cut history clause list R8 reads is PRESENT on the normative
#       page (specification.html) whenever the newest series has more than one
#       cut — R8 is a rule about a list it finds, and until K-1236 nothing said
#       WHICH file has to carry one (see the R8 limits below)
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT CHECKED, and why
#
#   * THE CUTS THEMSELVES AND THE ALIAS. A published cut is immutable (pass 1)
#     and the alias is byte-identical to the newest cut (pass 3), so a prose
#     rule over them could only be satisfied by mutating a frozen artefact —
#     a guard must never demand an edit the guard next to it forbids. A stale
#     example pin inside the skill text is fixed in the NEXT cut, which is
#     exactly what «bump first, then edit inside the new cut» is for.
#   * BARE FILENAMES IN PROSE (`skill-v0.4.1.md` without the origin). Those are
#     how this site refers to HISTORY — «skill-v0.4.1.md did exactly that: it
#     moved the cursor into a Link header» must keep its number forever. Only
#     the URL form is a pin, so only the URL form is held to the newest cut.
#     R5 still applies, so a bare name cannot dangle.
#   * skills.md's TABLE ROWS and CHANGELOG.md. Their job IS to name every cut;
#     R4 would make them unwritable. R5, R6 and R7 cover them instead. Note
#     ROWS: the exemption used to be per FILE, and skills.md's prose under the
#     table is not history — it states what the current cut is, in two sentences
#     every cut moves, and the file-level exemption handed them the table's
#     licence. See HISTORY_LINES.
#   * THE CURRENT-CUT RULES INSIDE CHANGELOG.md (K-1228, widened by K-1229). A
#     changelog entry is a record of a wave that has already happened, and this
#     repo's history is append-only, so an entry writing a concrete «a 0.4.10
#     operator serves», «0.4.0-0.4.9 describe earlier 0.4 cuts» or «0.4.3 →
#     0.4.10» is green the day it is written and RED ON THE VERY NEXT CUT — with
#     no legal repair, because the only conformant outcomes are a permanently red
#     gate or an edit the constitution forbids. That is the same reasoning that
#     keeps the cuts themselves out of pass 4: a guard must never demand an edit
#     the guard next to it forbids. The exemption covers exactly the rules whose
#     subject is WHICH CUT SET IS CURRENT — R1, R2, R3, R8 and R10 — and
#     CHANGELOG.md alone; see CLAIM_HISTORY_LINES for what is deliberately NOT
#     in it.
#   * WHICH CLAUSE OF THE R8 LIST BELONGS TO WHICH CUT (K-1236). R8 derives the
#     SET and the ORDER of the cut NUMBERS; it says nothing about the prose each
#     number carries. MEASURED by plant-and-restore (copy first; specification.html
#     sha256 557ce1c2… re-verified after): SWAPPING the descriptive clauses of
#     0.4.8 and 0.4.9 while leaving both numbers in place — so 0.4.8 now carries
#     «answering that second half wrongly» and 0.4.9 carries «stating the heavy
#     default toll's cost as a portable figure» — leaves this gate, its
#     --self-test AND check-publication-paths at EXIT 0. R8's own failure text
#     promises that «a clause written against the wrong cut tells them the wrong
#     thing about two», and that is TRUE ONLY WHEN THE NUMBER IS WRONG; read the
#     sentence as scoped to the number.
#     WHY IT IS STATED RATHER THAN CLOSED, and this is a judgement, not a fact:
#     a cut wave APPENDS a clause, it does not transpose two existing ones, so
#     no wave this list has ever seen produces the shape — where the two shapes
#     R8 does catch are both things a real wave did. And the only available fix
#     binds each clause to a phrase derived from its own cut (its CHANGELOG
#     line, say), which is a LEXICAL rule over prose — exactly the construction
#     EARLIER_WIRES was, and it matched nothing anywhere for its whole existence
#     while reading as coverage. A rule that fires on every rewording of a
#     sentence a human is meant to edit is worse than a stated limit. If a wave
#     ever IS found to have transposed two clauses, that is the evidence this
#     paragraph is waiting for and the trade changes.
#   * THE SPELLING OF R10's SPAN (K-1236). CHAIN_SPAN requires «→» or «->».
#     MEASURED by plant-and-restore (skills.md sha256 48b6cd01… re-verified):
#     re-spelling the live span as «0.4.3–0.4.9» (en-dash) or «0.4.3 to 0.4.9»,
#     STALE, leaves the gate at EXIT 0 — the pattern simply stops matching.
#     What catches it is the --self-test's VACUITY arm (EXIT 1, «chain_span
#     matches NOTHING in this repository»), because that span is the only
#     subject R10 has; see the CHAIN_SPAN comment for why widening the pattern
#     to those spellings is NOT the cheap fix it looks like.
#
# THE FOURTH ITERATION OF ONE FAILURE (K-1213 → K-1223 → K-1227). Three times
# running, a claim about what holds a relink was written without planting a
# break to test it, and three times it was wrong. Pass 4's own patterns were the
# fourth: `SERVES_CLAIM` matched only the negative wording, `EARLIER_WIRES`
# required a noun neither live sentence uses (so it matched nothing at all,
# anywhere, for as long as it existed), and skills.md was exempt from R4 by
# file. A faithful stale-relink planted in any of the four resulting classes
# left ALL SIX candidate mechanisms in this workspace at exit 0. The repair is
# in the patterns below, and `--self-test`'s VACUITY arm is what stops the class
# from recurring: a rule that matches nothing in this repository is now a
# failure rather than a green run.
PROSE_EXTENSIONS = %w[.md .html .json .txt].freeze

# THE HISTORY EXEMPTION IS PER-LINE, NOT PER-FILE (K-1227).
#
# Two files legitimately write cut names and pin URLs that are not the newest:
# skills.md enumerates every published cut, and CHANGELOG.md records what each
# wave did. Saying that BY FILE was the cheap way to put it, and it was wrong
# about skills.md. The table ROWS are history; the prose UNDER the table states
# what the current cut IS -- "0.4.3 = ... = 0.4.10", "only the second group
# describes what a 0.4.10 operator serves" -- and a file-level exemption handed
# that prose the table's licence. Every cut moves both of those sentences, and
# a plant in either left all six candidate mechanisms in this workspace at exit
# 0. So the exemption is a predicate over LINES: CHANGELOG.md is history end to
# end, skills.md exempts its table rows and nothing else.
HISTORY_LINES = {
  "skills.md" => ->(line) { line.start_with?("|") },
  "CHANGELOG.md" => ->(_line) { true }
}.freeze

# THE CURRENT-CUT RULES' history exemption, and it is NARROWER than the one
# above ON PURPOSE (K-1228, widened to R2/R3/R8/R10 by K-1229).
#
# WHY IT EXISTS: R4 and R7 are not the only rules an append-only file cannot
# satisfy. A CHANGELOG entry saying «a 0.4.10 operator serves the new wire»
# passes on the day it is written and fails on the next cut, and by then the
# entry is published history that must not be rewritten — so the tree can only
# come to rest red, or be repaired by an edit the constitution forbids. The
# K-1227 wave's own entry hit exactly this and was reworded to `0.4.N` before
# it was committed; a convention that every future author remembers to do that
# is precisely the kind of thing this workspace keeps learning does not hold.
#
# WHAT IS IN IT, and why the membership grew rather than the file list (K-1229):
# every rule whose subject is WHICH CUT SET IS CURRENT carries that unrepairable
# shape, not just R1. «0.4.0-0.4.9 describe earlier 0.4 cuts» (R2), «the 0.1.1-
# 0.3.11 cuts describe protocol 0.1-0.3» (R3), a per-cut history list (R8) and a
# «0.4.3 → 0.4.10» span (R10) are all green when written into a changelog entry
# and red on the next cut. K-1228 scoped its fix to R1 because nothing at head
# tripped the others and an exemption widened past the evidence stops meaning
# anything; K-1229 is the recorded decision to widen it, and the widening is one
# constant — the FILE list did not move.
#
# WHY IT IS SEPARATE FROM HISTORY_LINES RATHER THAN THE SAME MAP:
#   * `skills.md` is deliberately ABSENT, still. Its table rows are history for
#     PINS — naming every cut is the table's job — but a table cell that said
#     which cut is CURRENT would be a live claim, and handing these rules the
#     table's licence is the file-level mistake K-1227 spent a wave undoing.
#     R9 and R10 both READ those rows, and both find a live break there today.
#   * R5 is NOT exempted. It only asks that a named cut exists, which stays true
#     forever (cuts are never deleted — pass 1), so a changelog entry naming an
#     old cut can satisfy it in perpetuity and needs no licence.
#   * R6 and R9 are not exempted either: the cut table is not append-only in the
#     sense that matters here — a NEW cut adds a NEW row, and no existing row has
#     to change for either rule to stay satisfied.
# Adding a rule here is therefore a decision with a reason, not a default.
CLAIM_HISTORY_LINES = { "CHANGELOG.md" => ->(_line) { true } }.freeze

CUT_TABLE = "skills.md"

# A concrete cut named in text: `skill-v0.4.3.md`. Numeric on purpose — the
# placeholders this site writes on purpose (`skill-vX.Y.Z.md`,
# `skill-vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH.md`) must not be read as versions.
NAMED_CUT = /skill-v(\d+(?:\.\d+)+)\.md/
PIN_URL   = %r{https://kiosk\.tech/skill-v(\d+(?:\.\d+)+)\.md}

# A sentence stating which cut is current.
CURRENT_CLAIM = /
  (?: the\s+current\s+skill\s+is\s+v?
    | currently\s+skill-v
    | current\s+skill-v
    | newest\s+cut\s+is\s+v?
  )
  (\d+(?:\.\d+)+)
/xi

# "a 0.4.10 operator no longer serves" and "what a 0.4.10 operator serves" are
# ONE claim written from opposite ends, and both are live at head: protocol.md
# and specification.html write the negative, skills.md writes the positive. The
# pattern this replaces required the words "no longer", so the positive one —
# a sentence every cut moves — was held by nothing at all (K-1227).
SERVES_CLAIM = /a\s+(\d+(?:\.\d+)+)\s+operator\s+(?:no\s+longer\s+)?serves/i

# "0.4.0-0.4.9 describe earlier 0.4 cuts". THE TRAILING NOUN IS NOT PART OF THE
# PATTERN, on purpose: this rule used to require the literal word "wires", both
# live sentences say "cuts", and so it matched NOTHING ANYWHERE IN THE REPO
# while reading as though it covered them (K-1227). What identifies the claim is
# "<range> describe earlier <series>"; the noun after it is prose, and a rule
# keyed on prose is a rule one wording change switches off in silence. The
# self-test's VACUITY arm is the standing check that this cannot recur — a
# pattern that matches nothing here is now a failure rather than a green run.
EARLIER_WIRES     = /(\d+(?:\.\d+)+)\s*[-–—]\s*(\d+(?:\.\d+)+)\s+describe\s+earlier\s+(\d+\.\d+)(?![\d.])/i
EARLIER_PROTOCOLS = /(\d+(?:\.\d+)+)\s*[-–—]\s*(\d+(?:\.\d+)+)\s+(?:cuts\s+)?describe\s+protocol\s+(\d+\.\d+)\s*[-–—]\s*(\d+\.\d+)/i

# R7's subject: the cut table's same-wire chain, "0.4.3 = 0.4.4 = ... = 0.4.10".
# Three or more components per element so an ordinary "1.0 = 1.0" in prose is
# not read as one, and no capture group, so `scan` yields whole chains.
SAME_WIRE_CHAIN = /\d+(?:\.\d+){2,}(?:\s*=\s*\d+(?:\.\d+){2,})+/

# R8's subject: ONE CLAUSE of the per-cut history list that specification.html
# writes under the earlier-cuts range — «0.4.0–0.4.2 on wires that have since
# changed, 0.4.3 on this same wire but …, … and 0.4.9 on this same wire but …».
# EVERY cut moves that list (`git show --stat 00fd3ea`, `d29ebb6`), and until
# K-1232 NOTHING held it: deleting the newest clause, or writing it against the
# wrong cut, left all six candidate mechanisms in this workspace at exit 0.
#
# THE ANCHOR IS STRUCTURAL, NOT LEXICAL: a version (or a version range) followed
# by "on". Requiring the trailing noun is the mistake EARLIER_WIRES made — it
# demanded the word "wires" where both live sentences say "cuts" and so matched
# nothing anywhere for its whole existence. And note WHICH WAY this one fails: a
# reworded clause stops matching, which makes the enumerated list INCOMPLETE and
# the rule RED. A wording change cannot switch R8 off in silence; it can only
# make it complain. A future page that writes «0.4.5 on …» for some other
# purpose therefore goes RED and a human decides — the safe direction.
#
# THE SUBJECT IS UNIQUE TODAY, AND R11 EXISTS BECAUSE THAT IS NOT A GUARANTEE
# (K-1236). RE-MEASURED at head by running this file's own `plain` +
# CLAIM_HISTORY_LINES view over `prose_files` (57 files, tracked AND untracked
# .md/.html/.json/.txt): HISTORY_CLAUSE has a subject in exactly ONE file —
# specification.html, 8 clauses, all on ONE line, specification.html:57 — and
# CHAIN_SPAN in exactly ONE file, skills.md, 1 span, on ONE line, skills.md:24.
# Those two numbers are what the vacuity arm was silently resting on, and the
# arm asks a REPOSITORY-WIDE question — «does this rule match SOMEWHERE» — never
# «does it match the page that has to carry it». So before R11: deleting the
# WHOLE list from the normative page while any other prose file carried a
# conformant one left the gate AND the self-test at EXIT 0. MEASURED, by
# plant-and-restore: list cut out of specification.html + a decoy page
# enumerating 0.4.0-0.4.2 … 0.4.9 → gate EXIT 0, --self-test EXIT 0 (both files
# restored from `cp` copies, sha256 557ce1c2… / 48b6cd01… re-verified). R11 is
# the repair: the presence of the list is asserted of specification.html BY
# NAME, the way R6 asserts the cut table of skills.md, so it no longer depends
# on a uniqueness nobody was measuring.
HISTORY_CLAUSE = /(\d+(?:\.\d+)+)(?:\s*[-–—]\s*(\d+(?:\.\d+)+))?\s+on\s/

# R11's page. Hard-coded for the same reason CUT_TABLE is: «the list exists
# somewhere» is not the claim — specification.html is the NORMATIVE page, and
# the list is what tells an adopter why the cut they may be holding is
# superseded. Everything ABOUT the list stays derived from the cuts on disk.
HISTORY_PAGE = "specification.html"

# R10's subject: the same-wire group written as a SPAN instead of as a chain —
# skills.md's «the "same wire" chain below runs 0.4.3 → 0.4.9 only». R7 reads the
# "=" spelling; this is the same claim in the arrow spelling and must reach the
# newest cut for the same reason. Three-or-more components on both sides so
# "(0.3 → 0.4)" in prose is not read as one. MEASURED: the v0.4.8 and v0.4.9
# waves each moved this span; the v0.4.10 wave did not, and the sentence shipped
# a cut stale, because R7 cannot see the arrow and R4/R7's HISTORY_LINES exempt
# the row it lives on (K-1232).
#
# THE ARROW IS THE ONLY SPELLING THIS READS, AND WIDENING IT IS NOT CHEAP
# (K-1236). Re-spelling the live span «0.4.3–0.4.9» or «0.4.3 to 0.4.9», stale,
# leaves the gate at EXIT 0 (measured). But a dash or a « to » is ALSO how this
# site writes the two ranges that are stale BY DEFINITION: R2's «0.4.0–0.4.9
# describe earlier 0.4 cuts» and R3's «0.1.1–0.3.11 … protocol 0.1–0.3» both
# have three components on each side, so widening this pattern would demand that
# THEY reach the newest cut and turn two correct sentences permanently red. A
# guard must never demand an edit the guard next to it forbids, and R2 forbids
# exactly that edit. The limit stands, and what holds the class meanwhile is the
# --self-test's vacuity arm: this span is R10's only subject in the repository,
# so the re-spelling makes the arm's «matches NOTHING» fire (EXIT 1).
CHAIN_SPAN = /(\d+(?:\.\d+){2,})\s*(?:→|-+>)\s*(\d+(?:\.\d+){2,})/

# R7's ESCAPE, and the only one it has (T-158, K-1280). R7 requires the newest
# cut to appear at the end of a same-wire chain. That is unsatisfiable BY A TRUE
# SENTENCE the moment the newest cut OPENS a new wire group, because a chain
# needs two elements and the two candidates are both wrong: `0.4.12 = 0.4.12` is
# not a contiguous run of two published cuts and fails R7's own contiguity test
# (MEASURED), while `0.4.11 = 0.4.12` passes every test R7 has and SAYS THE
# FALSE THING -- that the cut which changed the wire describes the same wire as
# the one before it. R7's own failure message tells the author to "REWRITE the
# note if it opens a new group", and before this pattern existed there was no
# rewrite that both told the truth and left the gate green.
#
# So the reach requirement is satisfied by a chain ending at the newest cut OR
# by this sentence naming the newest cut, and NOTHING ELSE IS LOOSENED: every
# chain still has to be a contiguous run of published cuts, and the escape is
# itself a claim ABOUT THE NEWEST CUT, so it goes stale on the next cut exactly
# as the chain does. When 0.4.13 joins 0.4.12's group the chain `0.4.12 = 0.4.13`
# reaches the newest and the escape is not needed; when 0.4.13 opens a group of
# its own the sentence must name 0.4.13 or R7 fires. Either way a wave that
# moves nothing comes out red, which is the whole property K-1232 bought.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT IN VACUITY_RULES. The vacuity arm exists for rules that GATE
# -- a gating pattern with no subject is a gate switched off. This one is an
# ESCAPE: with no subject anywhere, R7 is simply in full force, which is the
# safe direction. Requiring it to match would demand this sentence in a
# repository that has never opened a second wire group.
NEW_GROUP = /\b(\d+(?:\.\d+){2,})\s+opens\s+a\s+new\s+wire\s+group\b/i

# R9's subject: one cut-table row naming exactly ONE cut, split into its version,
# its protocol cell and its body. The collapsed range rows («`skill-v0.3.0.md` …
# `skill-v0.3.11.md`») deliberately do not match: they describe a whole series
# and have no single predecessor.
CUT_TABLE_ROW = /^\|\s*`?skill-v(\d+(?:\.\d+)+)\.md`?\s*\|([^|]*)\|(.*)\|\s*$/

# R9's second half: «The same wire as 0.4.9.» at the head of a row body. The
# claim is RELATIVE — it names the row's own predecessor — so unlike R1 it is
# satisfied forever once written, and unlike R4 it is not history the table is
# licensed to keep: a NEW row written against the wrong cut is a stale relink
# that no other rule can see.
SAME_WIRE_AS = /wire\s+as\s+(\d+(?:\.\d+)+)/i

def cut_version(cut) = cut[/skill-v(.+)\.md\z/, 1]
def series_of(version) = version.split(".")[0, 2].join(".")
def version_order(version) = version.split(".").map(&:to_i)

# Tags, entities and markdown emphasis out of the way, so one set of patterns
# answers the same on `specification.html` and on `spec/protocol.md`.
def plain(text)
  text.gsub(/<[^>]+>/, " ")
      .gsub(/&(?:[a-zA-Z]+|#\d+);/, " ")
      .tr("*`", "  ")
      .gsub(/\s+/, " ")
end

# Everything pass 4 needs to know about the cuts on disk, derived once. Passed
# in rather than read from globals so the self-test can drive the same rules
# with a fixture set of cuts.
Ctx = Struct.new(:newest_cut, :newest_version, :newest_series, :all_versions, :series_cuts, :before_series)

def context_for(cut_files)
  newest  = cut_files.last
  version = cut_version(newest)
  series  = series_of(version)
  all     = cut_files.map { cut_version(_1) }
  Ctx.new(newest, version, series, all,
          all.select { series_of(_1) == series },
          all.reject { series_of(_1) == series })
end

# The part of a file a rule reads: everything except the lines whose job is to
# record history. The map is passed in because the exemptions are NOT the same
# for every rule — R4/R7 read HISTORY_LINES, R1 reads CLAIM_HISTORY_LINES — and
# a single shared map would have made K-1228's fix widen skills.md's table
# licence to R1 as a side effect.
def history_free(path, raw, exemptions = HISTORY_LINES)
  exempt = exemptions[File.basename(path)]
  return raw unless exempt

  raw.lines.reject { |line| exempt.call(line) }.join
end

# R7. The same-wire chain. Derived entirely from the cuts on disk: every element
# must be a published cut, the chain must be a CONTIGUOUS run of them, and the
# furthest a chain reaches must be the newest cut. Returns [failures, chains].
def chain_failures(path, text, ctx)
  fails  = []
  chains = text.scan(SAME_WIRE_CHAIN)
  return [fails, 0] if chains.empty?

  chains.each do |chain|
    named   = chain.scan(/\d+(?:\.\d+)+/)
    unknown = named.reject { ctx.all_versions.include?(_1) }
    unless unknown.empty?
      fails << "#{path}: the \"same wire\" chain \"#{chain}\" names #{unknown.join(", ")}, which " \
               "no published cut carries. Every element of that chain is a cut an assistant may " \
               "be holding, so a number nobody publishes cannot appear in it."
      next
    end

    idx = named.map { ctx.all_versions.index(_1) }
    next if idx.each_cons(2).all? { |a, b| b == a + 1 }

    gap = (idx.min..idx.max).map { ctx.all_versions[_1] } - named
    fails << "#{path}: the \"same wire\" chain \"#{chain}\" skips #{gap.join(", ")}. A chain reads " \
             "as \"every cut from here to here describes one wire\", so a hole in it either states " \
             "something false or hides a cut an adopter cannot place."
  end

  reach = chains.map { _1.scan(/\d+(?:\.\d+)+/).max_by { |v| version_order(v) } }
                .max_by { |v| version_order(v) }
  # The escape: a cut that OPENS a new wire group cannot end a chain truthfully,
  # so an explicit sentence naming it satisfies the reach requirement instead.
  # See NEW_GROUP for why this is not a loosening.
  opens_new_group = text.scan(NEW_GROUP).flatten.include?(ctx.newest_version)
  unless reach == ctx.newest_version || opens_new_group
    fails << "#{path}: the furthest a \"same wire\" chain reaches is #{reach}, but the newest cut " \
             "is #{ctx.newest_version} (#{ctx.newest_cut}). This note is the published answer to " \
             "\"can this cut talk to this operator\", so the newest cut must appear in it: extend " \
             "the chain if #{ctx.newest_version} describes the same wire, and if it does NOT, say " \
             "so in as many words — \"#{ctx.newest_version} opens a new wire group\" — which is the " \
             "one sentence this rule accepts in place of a chain, because a new group's first cut " \
             "cannot end one without claiming it is the same wire as its predecessor. Relinking is " \
             "PART of a skill change (K-847 rule 2)."
  end

  [fails, chains.size]
end

# R8. The per-cut history clause list, derived END TO END from the cuts on disk:
# every cut on the newest series BELOW the newest appears exactly once, in
# ascending order, and nothing else appears at all. Ranges are expanded THROUGH
# the published cuts, so «0.4.0–0.4.2» is three cuts and not two — which is why
# this cannot be done by counting clauses.
#
# What it catches, and both shapes were measured invisible to all six candidate
# mechanisms before it existed (K-1232): the wave that appends the cut and moves
# the R2 range but FORGETS the clause (the list then stops one cut short), and
# the wave that writes the clause against the WRONG cut (the list then names one
# twice and one not at all).
def history_clause_failures(path, clauses, ctx)
  want = ctx.series_cuts[0..-2].to_a
  if want.empty?
    return ["#{path}: enumerates cuts earlier than #{ctx.newest_version} on the " \
            "#{ctx.newest_series} series, but #{ctx.newest_cut} is the only cut on it. Reword " \
            "or remove the list."]
  end

  named = []
  clauses.each do |from, to|
    unknown = [from, to].compact.reject { ctx.all_versions.include?(_1) }
    unless unknown.empty?
      return ["#{path}: the per-cut history list names #{unknown.join(", ")}, which no published " \
              "cut carries. Every clause of that list is about a cut an assistant may still be " \
              "holding, so a number nobody publishes cannot appear in it."]
    end
    next named << from unless to

    lo, hi = ctx.all_versions.index(from), ctx.all_versions.index(to)
    if lo > hi
      return ["#{path}: the per-cut history list writes the range #{from}-#{to} backwards."]
    end

    named.concat(ctx.all_versions[lo..hi])
  end

  return [] if named == want

  ["#{path}: the per-cut history list reads #{named.join(", ")} — derived from the cuts on disk " \
   "it must read #{want.join(", ")}: every cut on the #{ctx.newest_series} series below " \
   "#{ctx.newest_version}, each exactly once, in ascending order. Every cut moves this list, " \
   "and relinking is PART of a skill change, not follow-up (K-847 rule 2): a clause that is " \
   "missing tells an adopter one published cut simply does not exist, and a clause written " \
   "against the wrong cut tells them the wrong thing about two."]
end

# R11. R8 reads a list WHERE IT FINDS ONE. Nothing said which file has to carry
# one, and the vacuity arm's question is repository-wide, so the normative page
# could lose the list entirely while both stayed green as long as any other
# prose file wrote the shape (K-1236, measured). This asserts the presence of
# the list on specification.html by name — the way R6 asserts the cut table of
# skills.md — and asserts nothing about its content, which stays R8's job.
#
# CONDITIONAL ON PURPOSE: when the newest series has only one cut there is no
# earlier cut to enumerate, and history_clause_failures says so and asks for the
# list to be REMOVED. Requiring its presence unconditionally would then demand
# an edit the rule next to it forbids — the trap CLAIM_HISTORY_LINES exists for.
def history_page_failures(text, ctx)
  return [] if ctx.series_cuts.size < 2
  return [] unless plain(text).scan(HISTORY_CLAUSE).empty?

  ["#{HISTORY_PAGE}: carries no per-cut history list at all, but #{ctx.series_cuts.size} cuts " \
   "are published on the #{ctx.newest_series} series. This page is NORMATIVE, and that list is " \
   "what tells an adopter holding #{ctx.series_cuts[0..-2].join(", ")} why the cut they have is " \
   "superseded. R8 checks a list where it finds one; only this rule says the list has to be " \
   "HERE (K-1236) — before it existed, deleting the list outright was invisible to every " \
   "mechanism in this workspace whenever some other page happened to write the same shape."]
end

# R1-R5, R7, R8 and R10 over ONE file's bytes. Returns [failures, claims checked,
# rules that found a subject here] — the third is what the self-test's vacuity arm
# reads, so a rule that matches nothing anywhere cannot sit green again.
def prose_failures(path, raw, ctx)
  fails  = []
  claims = 0
  hits   = []
  # R1, R2, R3, R8 and R10 read the claim-exempt view: a changelog records past
  # waves, and the sentence it records cannot be rewritten when the next cut
  # lands (K-1228, widened by K-1229). R5 and the HISTORY_LINES rules below
  # still read the whole file / their own exemption.
  claim_text = plain(history_free(path, raw, CLAIM_HISTORY_LINES))

  claim_text.scan(CURRENT_CLAIM) do |(named)|
    claims += 1
    hits |= [:current_claim]
    next if named == ctx.newest_version

    fails << "#{path}: names #{named} as the current skill; the newest cut is " \
             "#{ctx.newest_version} (#{ctx.newest_cut}). Relinking is PART of a skill change, not " \
             "follow-up (K-847 rule 2) — a version line on a published page tells an " \
             "adopter which cut to pin."
  end

  claim_text.scan(SERVES_CLAIM) do |(named)|
    claims += 1
    hits |= [:serves_claim]
    next if named == ctx.newest_version

    fails << "#{path}: says what a #{named} operator serves (or no longer serves), but the newest " \
             "cut is #{ctx.newest_version}. That sentence names the CURRENT cut whichever way " \
             "round it is written."
  end

  claim_text.scan(EARLIER_WIRES) do |(from, to, series)|
    claims += 1
    hits |= [:earlier_wires]
    if ctx.series_cuts.size < 2
      fails << "#{path}: describes cuts earlier than #{ctx.newest_version} within the " \
               "#{ctx.newest_series} series, but #{ctx.newest_cut} is the only cut on it. Reword " \
               "or remove the sentence."
      next
    end
    want_from, want_to = ctx.series_cuts.first, ctx.series_cuts[-2]
    next if from == want_from && to == want_to && series == ctx.newest_series

    fails << "#{path}: \"#{from}-#{to} describe earlier #{series} …\" — derived from the cuts on " \
             "disk that range is #{want_from}-#{want_to} on the #{ctx.newest_series} series " \
             "(every cut on the newest series except the newest itself)."
  end

  claim_text.scan(EARLIER_PROTOCOLS) do |(from, to, from_series, to_series)|
    claims += 1
    hits |= [:earlier_protocols]
    if ctx.before_series.empty?
      fails << "#{path}: describes cuts from protocol series older than #{ctx.newest_series}, " \
               "but every published cut is on #{ctx.newest_series}."
      next
    end
    want_from, want_to = ctx.all_versions.first, ctx.before_series.last
    next if from == want_from && to == want_to &&
            from_series == series_of(want_from) && to_series == series_of(want_to)

    fails << "#{path}: \"#{from}-#{to} describe protocol #{from_series}-#{to_series}\" — " \
             "derived from the cuts on disk that is #{want_from}-#{want_to}, protocol " \
             "#{series_of(want_from)}-#{series_of(want_to)} (every cut before the " \
             "#{ctx.newest_series} series)."
  end

  clauses = claim_text.scan(HISTORY_CLAUSE)
  unless clauses.empty?
    claims += clauses.size
    hits |= [:history_clause]
    fails.concat(history_clause_failures(path, clauses, ctx))
  end

  # R10 gets R7's escape, on the same reasoning and TIGHTER (T-158, K-1280).
  # When the newest cut opens a new wire group, the span describing the group
  # that just CLOSED truthfully ends one cut short, and demanding it reach the
  # newest would demand a false sentence. But the escape does not admit any
  # stale span: the span must end at the cut IMMEDIATELY BEFORE the newest, so a
  # span left at 0.4.9 while 0.4.12 opens a group is still red. R7 needs no such
  # bound because its chains are contiguity-checked already.
  opens_new_group = claim_text.scan(NEW_GROUP).flatten.include?(ctx.newest_version)
  predecessor     = ctx.all_versions[-2]
  claim_text.scan(CHAIN_SPAN) do |(from, to)|
    claims += 1
    hits |= [:chain_span]
    unknown = [from, to].reject { ctx.all_versions.include?(_1) }
    if !unknown.empty?
      fails << "#{path}: the same-wire span \"#{from} → #{to}\" names #{unknown.join(", ")}, " \
               "which no published cut carries."
    elsif to != ctx.newest_version && !(opens_new_group && to == predecessor)
      fails << "#{path}: the same-wire span \"#{from} → #{to}\" stops at #{to}, but the newest " \
               "cut is #{ctx.newest_version} (#{ctx.newest_cut}). A span written this way is the " \
               "same published claim as the \"=\" chain R7 reads — \"these cuts describe one " \
               "wire\" — so it must reach the newest cut: extend it if #{ctx.newest_version} " \
               "describes that same wire, and if it does NOT, say \"#{ctx.newest_version} opens " \
               "a new wire group\" in the same file and end this span at #{predecessor}, the cut " \
               "the closing group actually reaches."
    end
  end

  raw.scan(NAMED_CUT).flatten.uniq.each do |named|
    hits |= [:named_cut]
    next if ctx.all_versions.include?(named)

    fails << "#{path}: names skill-v#{named}.md, which does not exist. Every cut this site " \
             "names must be a file it publishes — a dangling pin fails closed on the " \
             "assistant's side and looks like the operator's fault."
  end

  free = history_free(path, raw)

  free.scan(PIN_URL).flatten.uniq.each do |named|
    claims += 1
    hits |= [:pin_url]
    next if named == ctx.newest_version

    fails << "#{path}: pins https://kiosk.tech/skill-v#{named}.md; the newest cut is " \
             "#{ctx.newest_cut}. A URL here is an EXAMPLE AN OPERATOR COPIES, so it must name " \
             "the cut this site would have them pin today. (A bare `skill-v#{named}.md` in " \
             "prose is history and is left alone — only the URL form is checked, and in " \
             "#{HISTORY_LINES.keys.join(" / ")} the cut-enumerating lines are exempt.)"
  end

  chain_fails, chain_count = chain_failures(path, plain(free), ctx)
  claims += chain_count
  hits |= [:same_wire_chain] if chain_count.positive?
  fails.concat(chain_fails)

  [fails, claims, hits]
end

# R6 and R9: the cut table. Older series are collapsed into ranges on purpose,
# so only the NEWEST series is required to carry a row per cut — that is the
# series an adopter chooses from.
#
# R6 holds the table's SHAPE — a row per cut, newest first — and until K-1232
# that was all anything held: the ROW BODY, which every cut writes fresh, was
# invisible. Changing «The same wire as 0.4.9» to «as 0.4.8», or a row's protocol
# column to a series its cut is not on, left every mechanism in this workspace at
# exit 0. R9 reads the body, and both of its claims are DERIVED: a row's protocol
# is `series_of` its own cut, and a «same wire as» in it names that cut's
# immediate predecessor among the published cuts. Both are RELATIVE claims, so
# unlike R1/R4 they are true forever once written — the table stays append-only
# and R9 still catches the row a wave writes against the wrong cut.
#
# Returns [failures, rules that found a subject here]; the second is what the
# self-test's vacuity arm reads.
def cut_table_failures(table, ctx)
  fails   = []
  hits    = []
  missing = ctx.series_cuts.reject { |v| table.include?("skill-v#{v}.md") }
  unless missing.empty?
    fails << "#{CUT_TABLE}: the cut table has no row for " \
             "#{missing.map { "skill-v#{_1}.md" }.join(", ")}. Every cut on the current " \
             "#{ctx.newest_series} series is listed there, newest first — a cut nobody can find " \
             "is a cut nobody adopts."
  end

  first_row = table[/^\|\s*`?skill-v(\d+(?:\.\d+)+)\.md/, 1]
  if first_row && first_row != ctx.newest_version
    fails << "#{CUT_TABLE}: the first row of the cut table is skill-v#{first_row}.md, but " \
             "the newest cut is #{ctx.newest_cut}. The table reads newest-first."
  end

  table.each_line do |line|
    row = CUT_TABLE_ROW.match(line)
    next unless row

    version, protocol_cell, body = row[1], row[2], row[3]
    next unless ctx.all_versions.include?(version) # R5 owns a name nobody publishes

    hits |= [:row_protocol]
    stated = protocol_cell.tr("*`", "  ").strip
    unless stated == series_of(version)
      fails << "#{CUT_TABLE}: the row for skill-v#{version}.md states protocol #{stated.inspect}, " \
               "but version parity puts that cut on #{series_of(version)}. This column is the " \
               "published answer to \"can this cut talk to this operator\" — the whole reason the " \
               "table exists — so it is derived from the cut's own name, not written by hand."
    end

    index    = ctx.all_versions.index(version)
    previous = index.positive? ? ctx.all_versions[index - 1] : nil
    body.scan(SAME_WIRE_AS) do |(named)|
      hits |= [:row_same_wire]
      next if named == previous

      fails << "#{CUT_TABLE}: the row for skill-v#{version}.md says it describes the same wire as " \
               "#{named}, but the cut published immediately before it is " \
               "#{previous || "(none — it is the oldest cut)"}. Each row states its cut's " \
               "relation to the one before it, and that is what makes the column readable as a " \
               "CHAIN (R7) rather than as a transitive licence; a row written against the wrong " \
               "cut silently joins two groups that are not the same wire."
    end
  end

  [fails, hits]
end

# Tracked files plus untracked-not-ignored ones: a new page that names a stale
# pin should fail before it is committed, not after.
def prose_files
  tracked, _err, _ok = git("ls-files", "-z")
  others,  _err2, _ok2 = git("ls-files", "--others", "--exclude-standard", "-z")
  (tracked.split("\0") + others.split("\0")).reject(&:empty?).uniq.select do |path|
    next false if path == ALIAS || CUT.match?(File.basename(path))

    PROSE_EXTENSIONS.include?(File.extname(path)) && File.file?(path)
  end.sort
end

if newest_cut
  ctx            = context_for(cuts)
  scanned        = prose_files
  claims_checked = 0

  scanned.each do |path|
    file_failures, file_claims, = prose_failures(path, File.read(path), ctx)
    failures.concat(file_failures)
    claims_checked += file_claims
  end

  if File.exist?(CUT_TABLE)
    table_failures, = cut_table_failures(File.read(CUT_TABLE), ctx)
    failures.concat(table_failures)
  else
    failures << "#{CUT_TABLE} is missing — it is the published index of every cut."
  end

  if File.exist?(HISTORY_PAGE)
    failures.concat(history_page_failures(File.read(HISTORY_PAGE), ctx))
  else
    failures << "#{HISTORY_PAGE} is missing — it is the normative page, and the per-cut history " \
                "list R8 reads lives on it."
  end

  notes << "prose: #{claims_checked} current-cut claim(s)/pin(s) checked against #{newest_cut} " \
           "across #{scanned.size} file(s)"
end


# ── --self-test ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS, and it is not symmetry with check-publication-paths.
#
# Pass 4 is a wall of regular expressions over prose, and the way one fails is
# not by throwing: it is by MATCHING NOTHING and exiting 0, which reads exactly
# like a clean tree. That is not hypothetical. `EARLIER_WIRES` required the
# literal word "wires", both sentences it exists for say "cuts", and it matched
# nothing anywhere in this repository for as long as it has existed;
# `SERVES_CLAIM` required "no longer serves" and skills.md writes the positive
# form; and skills.md's whole prose half was exempted along with its table. Each
# was a live class that every skill cut moves, and a faithful stale-relink plant
# in any of them left ALL SIX candidate mechanisms in the workspace green
# (K-1227, and K-1213/K-1223 before it — the third and fourth time a claim about
# what holds a relink turned out to be a claim nobody had planted against).
#
# So this has two arms, and the second is the one that answers K-1227:
#
#   FIXTURES — every rule is shown RED on a faithful stale-relink and GREEN on
#     the shape it must not fire on (a history row, a CHANGELOG line, the older
#     series' range).
#   VACUITY  — every rule must find a subject in THIS repository's own files. A
#     pattern with nothing to match is a pattern that has been switched off, and
#     from here that is a failure rather than a green run.
#
# Fixture cuts, not the real ones, so the fixtures state their own expectations
# and do not move on the next bump. The shape mirrors the real set: three older
# series, then a full 0.4.0..0.4.10 run.
FIXTURE_CUTS = (%w[0.1.1 0.2.0 0.3.11] + (0..10).map { "0.4.#{_1}" })
               .map { "skill-v#{_1}.md" }.freeze

# `overrides` replaces one row's protocol cell and body, so an R9 fixture is a
# WHOLE VALID TABLE with exactly one thing wrong in it — otherwise R6 would fire
# and the case would go red for the wrong reason.
def fixture_table(versions, overrides = {})
  "| Cut | Protocol | Wire it describes |\n|---|---|---|\n" +
    versions.map { |v|
      protocol, body = overrides.fetch(v, ["**0.4**", "some wire"])
      "| `skill-v#{v}.md` | #{protocol} | #{body} |\n"
    }.join
end

# [label, path, body, :red | :green]. The path decides which history exemption
# applies, so the same bytes are a violation in one file and history in another
# — which is the whole of what K-1227 got wrong.
def self_test_prose_cases
